                        "content": "     1\u2192\"\"\"Beancount syntax parser.\n     2\u2192\n     3\u2192IMPORTANT: The parser (and its grammar builder) produces \"incomplete\"\n     4\u2192Transaction objects. This means that some of the data can be found missing from\n     5\u2192the output of the parser and some of the data types vary slightly. Missing\n     6\u2192components are replaced not by None, but by a special constant 'NA' which helps\n     7\u2192diagnose problems if a user inadvertently attempts to work on an incomplete\n     8\u2192posting instead of a complete one. Those incomplete entries are then run through\n     9\u2192the \"booking\" routines which do two things simultaneously:\n    10\u2192\n    11\u21921. They find matching lots for reducing inventory positions, and\n    12\u21922. They interpolate missing numbers.\n    13\u2192\n    14\u2192In doing so they normalize the entries to \"complete\" entries by converting a\n    15\u2192position/lot's \"cost\" attribute from a CostSpec to a Cost. A Cost is similar to\n    16\u2192an Amount in that it shares \"number\" and \"currency\" attributes, but also has a\n    17\u2192label and a lot date. A CostSpec is similar to a Cost, but has all optional\n    18\u2192data; it consists in a specification for matching against a particular inventory\n    19\u2192lot.\n    20\u2192\n    21\u2192Other parts of a posting may also be missing, not just parts of the cost.\n    22\u2192Leaving out some parts of the input is used to invoke interpolation, to tell\n    23\u2192Beancount to automatically compute the missing numbers (if possible).\n    24\u2192\n    25\u2192Missing components will be set to the special value\n    26\u2192\"beancount.core.number.MISSING\" until inventory booking and number interpolation\n    27\u2192has been completed. The \"MISSING\" value should never appear in completed, loaded\n    28\u2192transaction postings.\n    29\u2192\n    30\u2192For instance, all of the units may be missing:\n    31\u2192\n    32\u2192  INPUT: Assets:Account\n    33\u2192  posting.units = MISSING\n    34\u2192\n    35\u2192Or just the number of the units:\n    36\u2192\n    37\u2192  INPUT: Assets:Account                    USD\n    38\u2192  posting.units = Amount(MISSING, \"USD\")\n    39\u2192\n    40\u2192You must always specify the currency.\n    41\u2192\n    42\u2192If a price annotation is simply absent, it appears as None:\n    43\u2192\n    44\u2192  INPUT: Assets:Account                 2 MXN\n    45\u2192  posting.price = None\n    46\u2192\n    47\u2192However, you may indicate that there is a price but have Beancount compute it\n    48\u2192automatically:\n    49\u2192\n    50\u2192  INPUT: Assets:Account                 2 MXN @\n    51\u2192  posting.price = Amount(MISSING, MISSING)\n    52\u2192\n    53\u2192Indicating the conversion currency is also possible (and recommended):\n    54\u2192\n    55\u2192  INPUT: Assets:Account                 2 MXN @ USD\n    56\u2192  posting.price = Amount(MISSING, \"USD\")\n    57\u2192\n    58\u2192If a cost specification is provided, a \"cost\" attribute it set but it does not\n    59\u2192refer to a Cost instance (as in complete entries) but rather to a CostSpec\n    60\u2192instance. Some of the fields of a CostSpec may be MISSING if they were not\n    61\u2192specified in the input. For example:\n    62\u2192\n    63\u2192  INPUT: Assets:Account  1 HOOL {100 # 5 USD}\n    64\u2192  posting.cost = CostSpec(Decimal(\"100\"), Decimal(\"5\"), \"USD\", None, None, False))\n    65\u2192\n    66\u2192Note how we never consider the label of date override to be MISSING; this is\n    67\u2192because those inputs are optional: A missing label is simply left unset in the\n    68\u2192computed Cost, and a missing date override uses the date of the transaction\n    69\u2192that contains the posting.\n    70\u2192\n    71\u2192You can indicate that there is a total number to be filled in like this:\n    72\u2192\n    73\u21